    <h1>Chapter 7: Embeddings & Vector Databases</h1>

    <div class="callout info">
      <div class="callout-title">📚 Chapter Overview</div>
      <p>Embeddings transform text, images, and other data into dense numerical vectors that capture semantic meaning. Vector databases enable efficient similarity search at scale, powering modern semantic search, recommendation systems, and RAG applications.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>What are Embeddings?</h2>

    <p>Embeddings are dense vector representations of data that capture semantic relationships:</p>

    <pre><code>Traditional (Sparse) Representation:
"cat" → [0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, ..., 0]  (one-hot: 10,000+ dimensions)

Embedding (Dense) Representation:
"cat" → [0.24, -0.18, 0.53, ..., -0.31]  (768 dimensions)
"dog" → [0.26, -0.15, 0.49, ..., -0.28]  (similar to cat!)
"car" → [-0.52, 0.31, -0.18, ..., 0.44]  (different from cat)

Key Properties:
• Dense, low-dimensional (typically 384-1536 dimensions)
• Semantically similar items have similar vectors
• Can measure similarity via cosine similarity or dot product
• Learned from data, not hand-crafted</code></pre>

    <h3>Why Embeddings Matter</h3>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Semantic Understanding:</strong> Capture meaning, not just keywords</li>
      <li><strong>Similarity Search:</strong> Find "cat" when searching "kitten"</li>
      <li><strong>Cross-modal:</strong> Text, images, audio in same space (CLIP)</li>
      <li><strong>Transfer Learning:</strong> Pre-trained embeddings work across domains</li>
      <li><strong>Efficient Computation:</strong> Vector operations are fast</li>
    </ul>

