    <h2>Similarity Metrics</h2>

    <h3>Cosine Similarity (Most Common)</h3>

    <pre><code>cosine_sim(A, B) = (A · B) / (||A|| * ||B||)

Range: [-1, 1]
• 1 = identical direction
• 0 = orthogonal (unrelated)
• -1 = opposite direction

Example:
A = [1, 2, 3]
B = [2, 4, 6]  # Scaled version of A
cosine_sim(A, B) = 1.0  # Perfect similarity

Why use it?
• Scale-invariant (magnitude doesn't matter)
• Works well for high-dimensional vectors
• Industry standard for text embeddings</code></pre>

    <h3>Other Similarity Metrics</h3>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Euclidean Distance (L2):</strong> Straight-line distance
        <ul>
          <li>d(A, B) = sqrt(Σ(Aᵢ - Bᵢ)²)</li>
          <li>Lower is more similar</li>
          <li>Sensitive to magnitude</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Dot Product:</strong> A · B = Σ(Aᵢ * Bᵢ)
        <ul>
          <li>Higher is more similar</li>
          <li>Fast to compute</li>
          <li>Used if embeddings are normalized</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Manhattan Distance (L1):</strong> Sum of absolute differences
        <ul>
          <li>d(A, B) = Σ|Aᵢ - Bᵢ|</li>
          <li>Less common for embeddings</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Vector Databases</h2>

    <p>Specialized databases optimized for storing and querying high-dimensional vectors at scale:</p>

    <h3>Why Not Use Traditional Databases?</h3>

    <pre><code>Challenge: Find k-nearest neighbors in high dimensions

Naive approach (compare to all vectors):
• 1M vectors * 1536 dimensions = 1.5B comparisons per query
• O(n) complexity - doesn't scale

Vector DB approach:
• Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) algorithms
• O(log n) or O(√n) complexity
• 10-100x faster with 95%+ accuracy</code></pre>

