    <h2>Chunking Strategies</h2>

    <p>How to split documents into embeddable pieces:</p>

    <h3>Why Chunk?</h3>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Context Window Limits:</strong> Embedding models have max input length (512-8192 tokens)</li>
      <li><strong>Semantic Granularity:</strong> Each chunk should contain coherent idea</li>
      <li><strong>Retrieval Precision:</strong> Smaller chunks = more precise matches</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Chunking Methods</h3>

    <pre><code>1. Fixed-Size Chunking
   • Split every N characters/tokens
   • Overlap: include last M tokens from previous chunk

   chunk_size = 1000 characters
   overlap = 200 characters

   Pros: Simple, predictable
   Cons: May split mid-sentence/paragraph

2. Sentence-Based Chunking
   • Use sentence boundaries
   • Group sentences up to max size

   Pros: Preserves sentence integrity
   Cons: Variable chunk sizes

3. Paragraph-Based Chunking
   • Split on paragraph boundaries
   • Maintain document structure

   Pros: Natural semantic units
   Cons: Paragraphs may be too large/small

4. Semantic Chunking
   • Use embeddings to find topic boundaries
   • Group semantically similar sentences

   Pros: Highest quality
   Cons: Computationally expensive

5. Recursive Chunking (LangChain default)
   • Try to split on: "\n\n" → "\n" → ". " → " "
   • Maintain hierarchy

   Pros: Balanced approach
   Cons: More complex</code></pre>

    <h3>Example: LangChain Text Splitter</h3>

    <pre><code class="language-python">from langchain.text_splitter import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter

text_splitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(
    chunk_size=1000,         # Target chunk size
    chunk_overlap=200,       # Overlap between chunks
    length_function=len,     # How to measure length
    separators=["\n\n", "\n", ". ", " ", ""]  # Split hierarchy
)

chunks = text_splitter.split_text(long_document)

# Each chunk is ~1000 chars with 200 char overlap
print(f"Created {len(chunks)} chunks")
print(f"Chunk 1: {chunks[0][:100]}...")</code></pre>

    <h3>Best Practices</h3>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Chunk Size:</strong> 500-1500 tokens is common sweet spot</li>
      <li><strong>Overlap:</strong> 10-20% overlap helps preserve context</li>
      <li><strong>Metadata:</strong> Store source, page number, section for better retrieval</li>
      <li><strong>Experiment:</strong> Test different strategies for your use case</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Hybrid Search</h2>

    <p>Combine vector search (semantic) with keyword search (lexical):</p>

    <pre><code>Vector Search (Semantic):
Query: "feline"
Finds: "cat", "kitten", "tiger" ✅

Keyword Search (Lexical - BM25):
Query: "feline"
Finds: only exact "feline" matches ❌

Hybrid Search:
Combines both approaches:
1. Get top K results from vector search
2. Get top K results from keyword search
3. Merge with Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)

score = Σ 1/(k + rank_i)

Benefits:
• Best of both worlds
• Handles rare terms, acronyms (keyword)
• Understands semantics (vector)
• 10-20% improvement in many cases</code></pre>

    <h3>Weaviate Hybrid Search Example</h3>

    <pre><code class="language-python"># Weaviate hybrid search
result = client.query.get(
    "Document", ["text"]
).with_hybrid(
    query="artificial intelligence",
    alpha=0.5  # 0 = pure keyword, 1 = pure vector, 0.5 = balanced
).with_limit(10).do()</code></pre>

    <h2>Filtering & Metadata</h2>

    <p>Combine vector search with structured filters:</p>

    <pre><code class="language-python"># Pinecone filtering
results = index.query(
    vector=query_embedding,
    top_k=10,
    filter={
        "category": {"$eq": "technology"},
        "date": {"$gte": "2024-01-01"},
        "author": {"$in": ["Alice", "Bob"]}
    }
)

# Qdrant filtering
from qdrant_client.models import Filter, FieldCondition, MatchValue

results = client.search(
    collection_name="my_collection",
    query_vector=query_embedding,
    query_filter=Filter(
        must=[
            FieldCondition(
                key="category",
                match=MatchValue(value="technology")
            )
        ]
    ),
    limit=10
)</code></pre>

    <h2>Production Considerations</h2>

    <h3>Performance Optimization</h3>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Batch Operations:</strong> Upsert/query in batches (100-1000 at a time)</li>
      <li><strong>Caching:</strong> Cache frequently accessed embeddings</li>
      <li><strong>Index Tuning:</strong> Adjust HNSW parameters (M, efConstruction, efSearch)</li>
      <li><strong>Quantization:</strong> Use scalar or product quantization for large datasets</li>
      <li><strong>Sharding:</strong> Distribute across multiple nodes for scale</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Monitoring & Evaluation</h3>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Recall@K:</strong> % of relevant docs in top K results</li>
      <li><strong>Precision@K:</strong> % of top K results that are relevant</li>
      <li><strong>Latency:</strong> Query response time (p50, p95, p99)</li>
      <li><strong>Relevance:</strong> Human evaluation, click-through rates</li>
    </ul>

